sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch11-12> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The constant angle of a shell's spiral, like its size, is no evidence of which form is more primitive or ancestral, nor does an intermediate angle show that a shell is intermediate in time or descent; phylogenetic series built from straight, loosely coiled and closely coiled cephalopod shells are not supported by the mathematical study of shell form.";
    dc:type "historical-inference";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Spiral constants of shells give no valid evidence of ancestry";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XI, “The Logarithmic Spiral”; topic “Spiral form and phylogeny”. Scope: cephalopod (nautiloid and ammonoid) shell forms and phylogenetic inference from them"
}
